\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Schematic of the gearbox analogy in the laboratory frame \(G\). The lower line represents the ground, which is stationary in this frame, and the arrow \(v_f\) denotes the speed of the gearbox/vehicle relative to the ground. Curved arrows indicate the sense of rotation, and the labels \(v_1\), \(v_2\), \(v_3\), and \(v_{\mathrm  {id}}\) denote local tangential speeds as defined in the text. The apparent sign mismatch at the upper rack--gear contact is resolved by the translation of the gearbox: because the gear center moves with the gearbox, the no-slip condition in the laboratory frame involves the \emph  {absolute} contact-point velocity, namely \(v_f-v_1\) at the upper contact, not the tangential contribution \(-v_1\) alone. (a) Positive transmission (downwind): the motion of the upper rack (wind) is transmitted through the gear train to the lower contact in the same sense, yielding velocity amplification. (b) Negative transmission (upwind): an idler gear reverses the transmitted sense of motion between the upper and lower contacts while preserving the same no-slip, ground-constrained coupling at the lower contact.}}{7}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gearboxes}{{3}{7}{Schematic of the gearbox analogy in the laboratory frame \(G\). The lower line represents the ground, which is stationary in this frame, and the arrow \(v_f\) denotes the speed of the gearbox/vehicle relative to the ground. Curved arrows indicate the sense of rotation, and the labels \(v_1\), \(v_2\), \(v_3\), and \(v_{\mathrm {id}}\) denote local tangential speeds as defined in the text. The apparent sign mismatch at the upper rack--gear contact is resolved by the translation of the gearbox: because the gear center moves with the gearbox, the no-slip condition in the laboratory frame involves the \emph {absolute} contact-point velocity, namely \(v_f-v_1\) at the upper contact, not the tangential contribution \(-v_1\) alone. (a) Positive transmission (downwind): the motion of the upper rack (wind) is transmitted through the gear train to the lower contact in the same sense, yielding velocity amplification. (b) Negative transmission (upwind): an idler gear reverses the transmitted sense of motion between the upper and lower contacts while preserving the same no-slip, ground-constrained coupling at the lower contact}{figure.3}{}}
\newlabel{eq:vf-vi-ratio}{{14}{7}{}{equation.14}{}}
\newlabel{eq:vaf-vf-vi-ratio}{{15}{7}{}{equation.15}{}}
\citation{Foxlin2021DWFTTW}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Dimensionless plots of two key relations used in the text. (a) In the gearbox analogy, with $x=r_2/r_3$, the ideal speed amplification $\frac  {v_f}{v_i}=\frac  {1}{1-x}$ increases as $x\to 1^{-}$, while the transmitted force ratio $\frac  {F_f}{F_i}=1-x$ decreases correspondingly. This illustrates the force--speed tradeoff of the lossless transmission. (b) In the vehicle frame \(C\), the apparent wind satisfies $\frac  {v_a}{v_w}=1-\frac  {v}{v_w}$. It is positive for $v<v_w$, vanishes at the crossover $v=v_w$, and becomes negative for $v>v_w$, meaning that the air then approaches from ahead in the vehicle frame.}}{8}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dimensionless_plots}{{4}{8}{Dimensionless plots of two key relations used in the text. (a) In the gearbox analogy, with $x=r_2/r_3$, the ideal speed amplification $\frac {v_f}{v_i}=\frac {1}{1-x}$ increases as $x\to 1^{-}$, while the transmitted force ratio $\frac {F_f}{F_i}=1-x$ decreases correspondingly. This illustrates the force--speed tradeoff of the lossless transmission. (b) In the vehicle frame \(C\), the apparent wind satisfies $\frac {v_a}{v_w}=1-\frac {v}{v_w}$. It is positive for $v<v_w$, vanishes at the crossover $v=v_w$, and becomes negative for $v>v_w$, meaning that the air then approaches from ahead in the vehicle frame}{figure.4}{}}
